SECTION 3 — Challenges & Future Improvements


3.1  Challenges Encountered During Development
The following issues were encountered and resolved during development. Each is a realistic representation of production MLOps challenges.

Challenge 1 — XGBoost Container Entry Point Failures
The SageMaker XGBoost framework container runs custom scripts with python -m module_name rather than python script.py. This means argparse behaves differently and any hyperparameter passed via the hyperparameters dict must be explicitly declared with add_argument(). Missing declarations cause a silent exit code: 1 with no traceback.
Resolution: Switched to the built-in XGBoost algorithm container which reads the CSV directly and accepts hyperparameters natively — eliminating the custom script entirely.
Challenge 2 — Stale Pipeline Definitions
SageMaker Pipelines caches the pipeline definition server-side. Calling pipeline.upsert() is supposed to update the definition, but in practice the next execution still used the old cached version. This caused multiple wasted training runs after code fixes.
Resolution: Added sm_client.delete_pipeline() before upsert() to force a clean recreation on every build.
Challenge 3 — Feature Column Mismatch in Batch Transform
The test CSV from preprocessing includes the label in column 0. The model was trained on 13 features only. Sending 14 columns to the batch container caused: 'num_feature (13) vs num_col (14)'.
Resolution: Added automatic label-column stripping before upload — reading the test CSV, slicing with iloc[:, 1:], and uploading the features-only version to a separate S3 prefix.
Challenge 4 — IAM Permissions in Lab Environment
The SageMaker execution role in the lab environment lacks cloudwatch:PutDashboard and sns:CreateTopic permissions. These are standard restrictions in shared lab accounts.
Resolution: Wrapped all optional IAM operations in try/except blocks with clear manual instructions. CloudWatch alarms (put_metric_alarm) worked; dashboard creation did not.
Challenge 5 — CSV Encoding
The Kaggle dataset uses ISO-8859-1 (Latin-1) encoding with non-breaking spaces (0xa0) in column names. Python's default UTF-8 reader raised UnicodeDecodeError immediately.
Resolution: Added encoding='latin-1' to all pd.read_csv() calls and stripped \xa0 characters from column names with str.replace().
